    "final_report": "REAL: two distinct models; statistics computed with Python.\n\nMethod: chi-square test of independence  (data version v1; Python: scipy.stats.chi2_contingency(observed, correction=False); Cramer's V uses the same uncorrected chi-square)\nH0: sex and admission are independent versus H1: they are associated; α = 0.05\n\n| | Admitted | Rejected |\n|---|---|---|\n| Male | 1198 | 1493 |\n| Female | 557 | 1278 |\n\nn = 4526; chi-square(1) = 92.205; p < 0.0001; Cramer's V = 0.143 (no Yates continuity correction)\nDecision at α = 0.05: reject_H0\nDistribution checks (descriptive only, never used to choose the method): [{'check': 'expected_counts', 'min_expected_count': 711.5388864339373, 'share_cells_expected_below_5': 0.0, 'n_cells': 4}]\n\nA total of 4526 records were analysed. The test result is chi-square(1) = 92.205, p < 0.0001. At the 0.05 significance level, we reject H0 (independence) in favor of H1 (association). The strength of the association is indicated by Cramer's V = 0.143, which suggests a weak to moderate association between sex and admission. However, this association does not establish causation, as random allocation was not established.\n\nLimitations:\n- No confidence interval was computed for Cramer's V in this stage.\n- Random allocation was not established. This analysis describes an association and does not establish causation.",
